interpersonal focus where we're getting at that, which reflects more of the, you know, psychoanalytic object relations approach. And, you know, but again, it comes back to how and why is somebody a perfectionist, you know, perhaps it's driven by needs that are well suited at cognitive behavior therapy. And in the cases of where it might actually be the case that somebody does have a demanding parent, for instance, or somebody who's really got some toxic expectations, it's about managing how to get through that relationship and not internalize the negativity. And the negativity usually comes in intense criticism that is directed right at the self in a way that makes people, again, feel inadequate or not worthy. So, you know,
我们正在触及这一点，这更多地反映了精神分析客体关系方法。而且，你知道，但同样，它回到了一个人为什么是完美主义者的问题上，你知道，也许它是由认知行为疗法非常适合的需求驱动的。在某些情况下，确实有人有要求苛刻的父母，或者确实有一些有毒期望的人，这是关于管理如何度过那种关系，而不是内化消极情绪。消极情绪通常以直接针对自我的强烈批评形式出现，使人们再次感到不足或不值得。所以，你知道，

41
00:26:28.000 --> 00:27:05.000
so I guess the message here is that it depends really on what, what is at the root of the perfectionism. And also, when somebody actually has a life setting where they have to be perfect, here we'd be talking about adults, but, you know, it's very important with somebody with a high-stressed job where they're not allowed to make a mistake to learn how to keep it focused on the workplace and not internalize that and generalize that in terms of interactions on the home front. So if you're an air traffic controller, for example, you can't make any mistakes on your job, but you can make mistakes elsewhere in your life. Yes, you want to land that plane.
所以我猜这里的信息是，这确实取决于完美主义的根源是什么。而且，当某人确实处于必须完美的生活环境中时，这里我们谈论的是成年人，但是，你知道，对于一个不允许犯错的高压工作的人来说，学习如何将其集中在工作场所，而不是内化并将其推广到家庭前线的互动中是非常重要的。所以如果你是一名空中交通管制员，例如，你在工作中不能犯任何错误，但你可以在生活的其他地方犯错误。是的，你想让那架飞机着陆。

42
00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:31.000
So what can parents or adults do themselves if they sense that they're, you know, tending toward perfectionism that is maybe becoming a problem? What advice do you give folks, Dr. Zucker? And you're talking about adults who are experiencing their own perfectionism, not necessarily models as parents. Could go either way. All right. So in general, I always encourage parents to,
那么，如果父母或成年人感觉到他们，你知道，倾向于可能正在成为问题的完美主义，他们自己能做些什么？你给人们什么建议，扎克博士？你谈论的是正在经历自己完美主义的成年人，不一定是作为父母的榜样。可能是双向的。好的。所以总的来说，我总是鼓励父母

43
00:27:31.000 --> 00:28:07.000
model making mistakes and to put words to times when they're not doing their best. So, for example, sometimes I might be rushed and stressed and I'm sort of barking at everyone. And I try not to have these moments often. But when I do, I'll say, you can tell I'm really stressed, right? And you can see, I'm not handling myself well right now, right? You know, and so I think when parents say I messed up or I didn't handle that well. They're modeling that it's okay to make mistakes. And to say things like we don't need to be perfect. And then to mirror their child,
示范犯错，并为他们没有尽力的时候用语言表达出来。例如，有时我可能会匆忙和压力大，我有点对每个人大喊大叫。我尽量不经常有这些时刻。但当我这样做时，我会说：“你可以看出我压力很大，对吧？你可以看到，我现在处理得不好，对吧？”你知道，所以我认为当父母说“我搞砸了”或“我处理得不好”时。他们是在示范犯错是可以的。并说出诸如“我们不需要完美”之类的话。然后去镜像他们的孩子，

44
00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:34.000
which does actually highlight what Gorge just said about the relational part, to mirror their child for who they are and to say, you're not perfect and I love you. And the who of you, right, the person in there, the you of you, which is always there is someone I love. and it has nothing to do with your performance. So I do think we have to be modeling that, expressing that. And then for the adult, who is just on their own perfectionist,
